Twenty-five years after SheepShaver first shipped, you still cannot boot Mac OS 9.2 on it. In theory you can get there through workarounds -- [hacks](https://web.archive.org/web/20160311205107/http://www.xlr8yourmac.com/OS9/os_9_2_1_install_mods.html) that coax 9.2 into starting -- but not with full New World ROM support, not the way you would expect it to just work. That bothered me more than it probably should have.

What I wanted was SheepShaver's JIT compilation running Mac OS 9.2. The JIT is what makes it fast: it translates PowerPC instructions to native ARM64 at runtime, so emulated software actually feels like software rather than waiting. QEMU and UTM can get you to Mac OS 9 on Apple Silicon, but without a JIT for classic Mac workloads they are slow in a way that defeats the purpose. SheepShaver was the right tool. Mac OS 9.2 was the version I needed. Getting the two together, done properly, turned out to be a much harder problem than it looked.

The rabbit hole went deep.

My [history with classic Mac OS](/posts/20200208-its-been-20-years.html) was in desktop publishing: Quark 4 on Mac OS 7, PostScript ads that could lock the whole system, the peculiar texture of cooperative multitasking. By the time I was doing web work seriously, I was already on Mac OS X. What I had never touched was the programming side of it: what classic Mac development actually felt like, the toolchain, building something for the platform from scratch. I had always been curious about that.

There is a G4 in the basement that could theoretically address this. It is loud, takes up space, and draws real power. So I have been approaching it from modern conveniences instead.

Over the past year that has meant several experiments at once. [Classic-vibe-mac](https://github.com/khawkins98/classic-vibe-mac) compiles C source to a 68k Mac binary and runs it live in System 7.5.5 via WebAssembly Basilisk II; writing Mac programs without leaving the browser. [Scriptoscope](https://github.com/khawkins98/scriptoscope) is a Mac OS-style window manager and Kaleidoscope theme engine for the browser. [PDF-A-go-slim](/posts/20260216-90s-desktop-paradigm-browser-utilities/) borrowed the floating palette paradigm from the 1995 Mac OS Human Interface Guidelines and applied it to a modern browser utility. All of that was aesthetics and runtime at arm's length from the actual software.

[Macsurf](https://github.com/mplsllc/macsurf) pushed it closer -- a classic Mac app I wanted to run natively, which led me back to SheepShaver, which led to the problem above.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - A trail of classic Mac experiments converged on a specific problem: SheepShaver's JIT speed combined with Mac OS 9.2, done without hacks.
> - SheepShaver now boots Mac OS up to 9.0 to the Finder desktop on M-series Macs with a native AArch64 JIT. Working; not yet distributed.
> - The current frontier: Mac OS 9.2 requires proper New World ROM support. That is the active work.

## The detour

SheepShaver has a four-link fork chain: the original [cebix/macemu](https://github.com/cebix/macemu), community-maintained by [kanjitalk755](https://github.com/kanjitalk755/macemu), then an ARM64 JIT backend by [rcarmo](https://github.com/rcarmo/macemu-jit) targeting Linux ARM boards. Nobody had taken it to macOS Apple Silicon. Getting there first meant porting that JIT backend to a platform with tighter constraints: MAP_JIT for executable pages, Mach exception handling instead of POSIX signals, address space limits that interact with SheepShaver's unusual architecture in specific ways.

SheepShaver was designed in the late 1990s to run native PowerPC Mac code on native PowerPC Linux. There was originally nothing to emulate at all, just the Mac-specific hardware. That cooperative design is still baked in everywhere, and a lot of its assumptions about address space and memory layout needed rethinking for a modern macOS host.

Getting to 9.2 is a different layer of the problem. A lot of time has gone into understanding how the nanokernel interacts with the MMU, figuring out what needs to be properly modelled versus what can be safely sidestepped, and then circling back to do real implementations after those investigations proved the shortcuts would not hold. I had expected to draw more directly on existing work -- [dingusppc](https://github.com/dingusdev/dingusppc) and QEMU were both early references -- but QEMU turned out to be most valuable as a diagnostic tool rather than an architectural model. The biggest unexpected source of inspiration was [Dolphin](https://dolphin-emu.org/), the GameCube and Wii emulator: a completely different platform, but the same class of problem.

Much of this has been done in collaboration with an AI coding partner. I wrote about that working pattern in [let the AI worry about the code, you worry about the work](/posts/20260420-let-ai-worry-about-the-code/). This project pushed it further than I expected: less about delegating implementation tasks, more about managing a technical investigation across sessions -- knowing when to go deep, when to step back, when a hypothesis had been falsified and it was time to try something else. Project management skills translate into this kind of work more directly than I would have guessed.

The result: SheepShaver boots Mac OS up to 9.0 to the Finder desktop on M-series Macs, with a native AArch64 JIT on by default. There is also preliminary AltiVec support, opt-in for now, because Mac OS 8.6 and 9.0 do not save vector registers across task switches.

## What is next

The Apple Silicon JIT work was the prerequisite. Mac OS 9.2 is the actual goal. It is worth being honest about why 9.2 specifically: the features it adds over 9.0 -- preemptive multitasking, proper memory protection, 128-bit file encryption -- are not things I actually need. Nobody is running production workloads on a classic Mac. But most ambitious hobby projects do not have a compelling real-world justification; that is more or less what makes them hobby projects. The point is getting the thing right. That said, if this all comes together, [Final Cut Pro 3](https://support.apple.com/en-us/112424) on SheepShaver becomes possible. Which is entirely unnecessary and, I think, quite cool.

It requires a newer ROM (Mac OS ROM 9.0.1 rather than the 1998 v1.1 ROM) and that ROM needs a proper hardware emulation layer that SheepShaver never had: real device models for the SCC serial chip, VIA timer, ADB controller, and interrupt routing. That work is underway; it is what I expect to write more about as it develops.

Running alongside it: Silicon Sheep, a native macOS launcher for SheepShaver that makes the day-to-day experience less of a configuration exercise.

Neither is done. This post is less an announcement than a status note: here is what has been happening, here is what is in progress.

## On the rabbit hole

The README for the project opens with this:

> After waiting a quarter of a century, it became obvious that nobody else was going to get New World ROMs running just-in-time on Apple Silicon either. So I thought maybe I would.

I do not have formal domain expertise in emulator development. What I have is curiosity, some stubbornness, and an AI partner that turned out to be a good collaborator on gnarly low-level problems. A fair amount of that will not be to everyone's taste, and I understand that. It is a hobby project; I am sharing it back in case any of it is useful, not because anyone has to approve of the method.

How far this goes, I am not sure yet. But the Finder boots on my M-series Mac; that is further than it got in the preceding twenty-five years.

If you have been down a similar road, I would be glad to hear from you.

